WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.870
This name is not getting any easier, is it?
It used to be Károly Zsolnai, which was hard

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.710
enough, and now this... haha. Anyway, let's
get started.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:21.619
This technique simulates how different objects
in a video sound when struck. We have showcased

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:27.390
some marvelous previous techniques that were
mostly limited to wooden and plastic materials.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.470
Needless to say, there are links to these
episodes in the video description box.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:37.380
A convolutional neural network takes care
of understanding what is seen in the video.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:43.080
This technique is known to be particularly
suited to processing image and video content.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.040
And it works by looking at the silent video
directly and trying to understand what is

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.530
going on, just like a human would.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:56.250
We train these networks with input and output
pairs - the input is a video of us beating

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.530
the hell out of some object with a drumstick.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.000
The joys of research! And the output is the

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.319
sound this object emits.

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:18.229
However, the output sound is something that
changes in time. It is a sequence, therefore

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:24.119
it cannot be handled by a simple classical
neural network. It is learned by a recurrent

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:28.049
neural network that can take care of learning
such sequences.

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:32.429
If you haven't heard these terms before, no
worries, we have previous episodes on all

00:01:32.429 --> 00:01:35.959
of them in the video description box, make
sure to check them out!

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:42.399
This piece of work is a nice showcase of combining
two quite powerful techniques: the convolutional

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:48.159
neural network tries to understand what happens
in the input video, and the recurrent neural

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:53.859
network seals the deal by learning and guessing
the correct sound that the objects shown in

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:55.969
the video would emit when struck.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:01.869
The synthesized outputs were compared to the
real world results both mathematically and

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:07.439
by asking humans to try to tell from the two
samples which one the real deal is. These

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:13.780
people were fooled by the algorithm around
40% of the time, which I find to be a really

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:22.280
amazing result, considering two things: first,
the baseline is not 50%, but 0% because people

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:28.250
don't pick choices at random - we cannot reasonably
expect a synthesized sound to fool humans

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:33.970
at any time. Like nice little neural networks,
we've been trained to recognize these sounds

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:39.370
all our lives, after all. And second, this
is one of the first papers from a machine

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:45.600
learning angle on sound synthesis. Before
reading the paper, I expected at most 10 or

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.450
20 percent, if that.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.900
The tidal wave of machine learning runs through
a number of different scientific fields. Will

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.760
deep learning techniques establish supremacy
in these areas? Hard to say yet, but what

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.500
we know for sure is that great strides are
made literally every week. There are so many

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:10.210
works out there, sometimes I don't even know
where to start. Good times indeed!

